WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:01.393
- I'm X.

00:00:01.393 --> 00:00:02.226
- I'm Dixon.

00:00:02.226 --> 00:00:03.726
- And this is Academy Green Room.

00:00:03.726 --> 00:00:06.309
(upbeat music)

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:10.270
- It's a special day today.

00:00:10.270 --> 00:00:14.100
Firstly because I have on
makeup and if I have on makeup,

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:15.425
that means it's a special day today.

00:00:15.425 --> 00:00:16.480
- You know it's something real.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:17.383
- You know it's something real.

00:00:17.383 --> 00:00:20.190
It's also a special day because I'm here

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:21.830
with an Oscar nominee.

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:23.940
Oscar nominated for best song,

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:24.990
singer,

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:29.990
vocal arranger, producer,
musician, and now Oscar nominee

00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:32.380
for best song for "Be Alive."

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:37.380
Co-written and sung by none
other than Queen B, Beyonce.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:38.950
Dixon welcome man.

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:40.720
Peace, congratulations by the way.

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:41.553
- Thank you man.

00:00:41.553 --> 00:00:42.386
- How are you feeling?

00:00:42.386 --> 00:00:43.530
- I'm feeling good, man.

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:45.740
You know, it's this the big weekend.

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:47.390
- I just really want to
know your story, man.

00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:48.280
How did you get here?

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:49.876
And I don't mean, did you get a lift

00:00:49.876 --> 00:00:51.500
or did you get an Uber here,

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:54.130
or did Kenny back there at
home and drive you here?

00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:55.940
How did you get here?

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:58.940
- I mean just a lot of faith and patience.

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:03.760
Honestly, I can't really
take too much credit

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:05.350
for my own talents.

00:01:05.350 --> 00:01:09.330
I feel like those are a gift from God.

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:12.520
But patience is something
you have to learn,

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:15.300
and faith is something
you have to practice.

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:16.133
- That's real.

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:18.510
- I've done both of those things.

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:21.540
And so I think it's a
good time for those things

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:22.373
to start working.

00:01:22.373 --> 00:01:24.680
- All right, we better start
unpacking some of this stuff.

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:25.513
You gonna give some stuff

00:01:25.513 --> 00:01:26.346
and I'm gonna throw it right back to you.

00:01:26.346 --> 00:01:27.370
So you said patience.

00:01:27.370 --> 00:01:29.614
Tell me about something you went through

00:01:29.614 --> 00:01:31.793
that gave you patience
to get to this moment.

00:01:31.793 --> 00:01:34.900
Because I don't want
people to just see Dixon,

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:38.233
this amazing producer and
you're at the Oscars, right?

00:01:39.479 --> 00:01:41.600
It just didn't just happen this way.

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:44.250
Some things happen a along
the way to get you here.

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:48.570
- Yeah, I guess one
thing that happened was

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:53.570
I had to be in the background for a while.

00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:55.570
- How did that feel?

00:01:55.570 --> 00:01:58.280
- I mean, it felt great
because I was feeding my family

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:00.223
and taking care of myself.

00:02:01.684 --> 00:02:03.960
I don't ever complain about those moments

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:07.110
because they really shaped me and made me

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:09.343
the professional that I am.

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:16.820
To be quite honest I signed
a record deal back in 2019

00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:20.080
and I was still on the road
as a background singer.

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:23.740
So those two worlds
happening at the same time.

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:25.590
Working on my own solo projects,

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:29.170
and then supporting artists
who were ahead of me

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:32.870
in their careers, sometimes that dichotomy

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:37.870
was a struggle because
you believe in yourself

00:02:38.317 --> 00:02:41.020
and you want people to
see what you're doing

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:42.030
as a solo artist.

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:45.623
But it's like it hasn't
really taken off there yet.

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:48.050
So you gotta support.

00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:50.186
And I really don't sign up to do things

00:02:50.186 --> 00:02:52.327
that I don't fully believe in.

00:02:52.327 --> 00:02:54.950
And so the artists that I've
been fortunate to work with

00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:57.880
in a more background capacity,

00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:00.700
I believe in their work and
I've always been in service

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:01.620
to what they do.

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:03.430
And so it worked out for me.

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:07.717
Because that patience coupled
with a bit of humility made me

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:09.863
who I am.

00:03:09.863 --> 00:03:11.310
- How'd you get to that space where

00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:15.971
you were doing background vocals
for these different artists

00:03:15.971 --> 00:03:17.490
and who were some of these artists?

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:19.883
- I started off with Vic Mensa.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:23.510
Who's a label made of mine now,

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:25.340
and one of my really close friends.

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:29.710
I started off with him and
that was kind of on a whim.

00:03:29.710 --> 00:03:32.530
I met him through Pharrell.

00:03:32.530 --> 00:03:34.900
And Pharrell was like, you'all
need to know each other.

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:38.570
And he put out this project
called Autobiography.

00:03:38.570 --> 00:03:40.240
And I called and I was like,

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:43.920
do you got anybody singing
background with you on the road?

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:46.620
And really it was just, I
was trying to get out there.

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:48.850
And he didn't.

00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:52.332
And so I just showed up to rehearsal

00:03:52.332 --> 00:03:55.540
and had my little rig
going and he walked in

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:58.050
and it was just like a match.

00:03:58.050 --> 00:04:00.100
And so I toured with him for a while.

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:03.820
And through that I met
Chance, Chance the Rapper.

00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:07.361
- Y'all have adult collaboration
with Nicki Minaj as well.

00:04:07.361 --> 00:04:08.630
You wanna talk about that too?

00:04:08.630 --> 00:04:11.300
- Yeah I mean, I started working
with Chance in the studio.

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:14.080
I didn't plan to tour with him.

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:19.000
And honestly the real
story is around 2019,

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:20.720
my father had passed away in June.

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:21.553
- My condolences.

00:04:21.553 --> 00:04:22.923
- And thank you man, I appreciate that.

00:04:22.923 --> 00:04:26.860
And I knew I had spent all my money

00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:29.530
to bury my father basically.

00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:30.870
And I was like, I need work.

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:34.470
And so luckily the timing was right.

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:39.021
He called me to do one song,
just some vocal arrangement.

00:04:39.021 --> 00:04:41.960
And he loved it so much that
they invited me to Chicago

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:43.510
to work on the album.

00:04:43.510 --> 00:04:46.430
And so my plan was to literally work.

00:04:46.430 --> 00:04:49.270
He has an incredible
organization called Social Works.

00:04:49.270 --> 00:04:52.950
I was gonna work the
summer camp during the day

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:57.259
to make money, and at
night work on the album.

00:04:57.259 --> 00:04:58.819
- And we're talking about
less than three years ago.

00:04:58.819 --> 00:05:01.667
- Yeah, and I was fine with that.

00:05:01.667 --> 00:05:03.060
I told my girlfriend I was like,

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:06.540
listen, whatever I gotta do
to make sure the lights is on

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:07.600
I'll work in the summer camp,

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:09.260
be with the kids during the day.

00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:14.120
And then on night time hit
at the studio and just hope

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:15.240
I don't pass out at work.

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:16.380
- The next morning.

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:20.230
And it just kind of happened
that he wanted to tour.

00:05:20.230 --> 00:05:22.130
And so instead of doing the summer camp,

00:05:22.130 --> 00:05:23.932
I went from the studio
to just be on the road.

00:05:23.932 --> 00:05:28.273
I think my first show with him
was like in London somewhere.

00:05:28.273 --> 00:05:30.488
And I was in the studio
I get the call like,

00:05:30.488 --> 00:05:31.321
do you wanna go?

00:05:31.321 --> 00:05:35.170
And I was literally on a
plane to London to sing back

00:05:35.170 --> 00:05:38.000
and the rest has kind of
been history with him.

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:40.860
- So how did you get to the
point from almost working

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:44.970
at summer camp, being on a
road in London with Chance,

00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:48.610
doing stuff with Vic Mensa
and then getting to the point

00:05:48.610 --> 00:05:53.610
where you're producing and
co-writing with Beyonce.

00:05:53.827 --> 00:05:55.793
One of the biggest artists of all.

00:05:57.090 --> 00:05:59.490
- I can't really say it's
like a secret to this.

00:05:59.490 --> 00:06:04.490
It's just you gotta really
believe in what you do.

00:06:06.756 --> 00:06:10.493
and then commit to being in service to it.

00:06:12.010 --> 00:06:14.460
I tell people all the time
like I'm in service to music.

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:17.580
So when I get called to do something,

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:20.280
I'm fully a hundred percent in.

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:22.610
Like sign me up.

00:06:22.610 --> 00:06:24.310
Obviously if we say yes to the opportunity

00:06:24.310 --> 00:06:27.480
I try not to take too many
things on at one time.

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:30.240
Because my manager who's
off camera will tell you,

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:31.313
I tend to do that.

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:34.287
But when I'm in, I'm fully in.

00:06:34.287 --> 00:06:37.550
And so I've just been in
service and I made up in my mind

00:06:37.550 --> 00:06:39.120
during the pandemic I was like,

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:42.020
I'm just gonna keep
submitting songs to be.

00:06:42.020 --> 00:06:43.310
And see what happens.

00:06:43.310 --> 00:06:44.730
And so I would write something,

00:06:44.730 --> 00:06:47.020
I'd be like, okay that feels good for us.

00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:49.010
I would just send it off.

00:06:49.010 --> 00:06:51.048
And I didn't think she had heard anything.

00:06:51.048 --> 00:06:53.494
- I was gonna ask you, did
you ever get any feedback?

00:06:53.494 --> 00:06:54.600
- Not really anything
- Anything just

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:58.110
- Not really not until we
got the call for the movie.

00:06:58.110 --> 00:07:01.357
And I was like, oh she's heard things.

00:07:02.790 --> 00:07:07.790
And thinks I'm a good candidate
for writing this song.

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:12.749
And so immediately I just
dove in and we're here.

00:07:12.749 --> 00:07:15.030
- Did you bug out when you got the call?

00:07:15.030 --> 00:07:16.563
- I freaked out a bit.

00:07:17.522 --> 00:07:20.780
I take what I do seriously,

00:07:20.780 --> 00:07:22.568
but then you get some calls and it's like,

00:07:22.568 --> 00:07:25.118
you just kind of gotta
laugh about it, like really?

00:07:26.934 --> 00:07:28.134
It's super unbelievable.

00:07:29.866 --> 00:07:32.470
That was definitely one of those moments

00:07:32.470 --> 00:07:34.450
where I had to pinch myself.

00:07:34.450 --> 00:07:35.637
- And what was the process like?

00:07:35.637 --> 00:07:39.070
Like what was that process
of now you've gotten the call

00:07:39.070 --> 00:07:43.540
from Beyonce's camp and you
guys are actually sitting down

00:07:43.540 --> 00:07:46.390
to do the songwriting for
you to produce the record.

00:07:46.390 --> 00:07:47.383
What is that like?

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:51.470
- The process was quick, to be honest.

00:07:51.470 --> 00:07:56.463
We didn't have much time to get it in.

00:07:57.490 --> 00:08:02.483
So I came here to LA and
day one, I just did a demo.

00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:06.880
- You came up like, did
they talk about the concept?

00:08:06.880 --> 00:08:07.713
I wanna know.

00:08:07.713 --> 00:08:09.540
- I hadn't really seen much of the movie.

00:08:09.540 --> 00:08:11.310
- Okay, and did they
tell you about the movie?

00:08:11.310 --> 00:08:12.620
- I knew about the story.

00:08:12.620 --> 00:08:14.873
So I knew about obviously knew about it,

00:08:14.873 --> 00:08:18.730
Serena, Venus, and their
father and how much of a role

00:08:18.730 --> 00:08:20.200
he played in their lives.

00:08:20.200 --> 00:08:21.333
I saw the trailer,

00:08:22.241 --> 00:08:25.950
and that's when everything
started to hit me.

00:08:25.950 --> 00:08:29.860
Because if you know Beyonce's story,

00:08:29.860 --> 00:08:31.890
she also had a father who
was very instrumental.

00:08:31.890 --> 00:08:32.850
- Strong black father.

00:08:32.850 --> 00:08:34.250
- In her life.

00:08:34.250 --> 00:08:37.297
I myself had a very
strong father in my life.

00:08:37.297 --> 00:08:40.099
- You mentioned him off camera,
but when we hadn't started.

00:08:40.099 --> 00:08:44.470
- Yeah, so he's a quite
instrumental in my life

00:08:44.470 --> 00:08:47.850
and how I approach my life.

00:08:47.850 --> 00:08:52.010
I take a lot of what he taught
me with me on a daily basis.

00:08:52.010 --> 00:08:54.440
And so I knew this part of
the story and I was like,

00:08:54.440 --> 00:08:57.290
well this part will be
the easiest part to write.

00:08:57.290 --> 00:08:59.790
Now we just have to make something

00:08:59.790 --> 00:09:02.757
that is captivating enough
for the directors of

00:09:02.757 --> 00:09:04.163
the film and the studio.

00:09:04.163 --> 00:09:07.400
- And they feel like it
should be a part of a movie.

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:11.850
- Yeah, and so it really
just started with guitars,

00:09:11.850 --> 00:09:15.072
me sitting down on a couch
and just plucking through like

00:09:15.072 --> 00:09:18.150
the first initial versus.

00:09:18.150 --> 00:09:19.630
I recorded the demo.

00:09:19.630 --> 00:09:22.820
I want to say it took me
about like two or three hours

00:09:22.820 --> 00:09:23.653
and I felt good.

00:09:23.653 --> 00:09:25.070
I was going to bounce it out,

00:09:25.070 --> 00:09:28.380
take it home, listen to
it, make my own notes.

00:09:28.380 --> 00:09:33.163
And then I see around the
studio some commotion happening

00:09:33.163 --> 00:09:35.330
and I'm like, oh my God.

00:09:35.330 --> 00:09:36.550
- Now what was this commotion?

00:09:36.550 --> 00:09:40.794
- Well, she came to the
studio while I was working.

00:09:40.794 --> 00:09:44.043
- And I wasn't quite done with the record.

00:09:44.043 --> 00:09:45.756
I mean looking back on it,

00:09:45.756 --> 00:09:47.457
I was more done than I thought I was.

00:09:47.457 --> 00:09:50.150
But as creatives a lot of
times we get in our head.

00:09:50.150 --> 00:09:52.420
- You're working on forever.

00:09:52.420 --> 00:09:54.065
Let's talk it.

00:09:54.065 --> 00:09:56.200
Somebody gotta snatch the record at you.

00:09:56.200 --> 00:09:57.500
- Kenny often is like,

00:09:57.500 --> 00:09:59.400
no, you're gonna release this version.

00:10:00.370 --> 00:10:03.110
But now she came while I was working on it

00:10:03.110 --> 00:10:04.422
and I played it for it her.

00:10:04.422 --> 00:10:07.380
But I didn't look at her the whole time

00:10:07.380 --> 00:10:08.213
that I was playing for her.

00:10:08.213 --> 00:10:09.090
- But that's awkward too.

00:10:09.090 --> 00:10:11.010
When you're playing music for somebody

00:10:11.010 --> 00:10:11.907
to be the creator of it.

00:10:11.907 --> 00:10:13.580
I've been on the other side.

00:10:13.580 --> 00:10:16.610
So I've been the person
in the room so many times,

00:10:16.610 --> 00:10:19.150
working at Spotify,
working at record labels.

00:10:19.150 --> 00:10:20.870
And I'm in there listening to the music

00:10:20.870 --> 00:10:22.440
and the artist is there and I'm like,

00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:24.572
damn, this is awkward for both of us.

00:10:24.572 --> 00:10:25.405
You know what I mean?

00:10:25.405 --> 00:10:27.500
And you're like, how hard do I nod my head

00:10:27.500 --> 00:10:29.740
or do I look at them
while I'm listening to it?

00:10:29.740 --> 00:10:31.983
So it's usually this, you kind of nod.

00:10:31.983 --> 00:10:33.163
- I didn't turn that
chair around one time.

00:10:33.163 --> 00:10:36.620
I was also very nervous
because I hadn't bounced

00:10:36.620 --> 00:10:38.238
the song out of pro tools yet.

00:10:38.238 --> 00:10:39.071
So I was nervous.

00:10:39.071 --> 00:10:40.542
I was gonna press the wrong button

00:10:40.542 --> 00:10:42.680
if made any some movements.

00:10:42.680 --> 00:10:44.700
It wasn't my personal gear either.

00:10:44.700 --> 00:10:46.400
So I didn't feel very comfortable.

00:10:47.613 --> 00:10:49.350
So I didn't move.

00:10:49.350 --> 00:10:50.492
And when I turned around,

00:10:50.492 --> 00:10:52.700
she had a big smile on her face

00:10:52.700 --> 00:10:55.410
and she immediately came out of the gate,

00:10:55.410 --> 00:10:56.940
giving me notes and everything else.

00:10:56.940 --> 00:11:01.940
So we just first demoed
down and she gave her notes

00:11:02.153 --> 00:11:04.000
and then took the record with her

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:06.640
and did her own rewrites
and reproductions.

00:11:06.640 --> 00:11:07.910
And then I got it back.

00:11:07.910 --> 00:11:11.993
And I mean when I got it
back, it was so magical.

00:11:12.920 --> 00:11:15.740
But then I was encouraged to keep digging

00:11:15.740 --> 00:11:18.580
and keep carving at it to
make sure her that it was what

00:11:18.580 --> 00:11:21.060
I wanted also.

00:11:21.060 --> 00:11:22.490
I think that's what's really special

00:11:22.490 --> 00:11:24.480
about working with Beyonce.

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:26.745
Honestly people don't talk enough about

00:11:26.745 --> 00:11:30.300
who she is as a songwriter and a producer.

00:11:30.300 --> 00:11:34.520
But I also think it's very
generous of her to allow me

00:11:34.520 --> 00:11:38.870
to keep carving at it when she's
so good at what's she does.

00:11:38.870 --> 00:11:41.080
Honestly, we have these legacy artists

00:11:41.080 --> 00:11:45.370
and we hear all these
stories about how tough it is

00:11:45.370 --> 00:11:47.400
to work with them, for them.

00:11:47.400 --> 00:11:50.470
- They won't bend or they
won't accept like feedback

00:11:50.470 --> 00:11:51.800
or collaboration way.

00:11:51.800 --> 00:11:53.130
It's like, no, I got it from here.

00:11:53.130 --> 00:11:56.300
- Yeah, but I think B
is a creative honestly,

00:11:56.300 --> 00:11:58.620
and she respects other creatives.

00:11:58.620 --> 00:12:02.040
And I think that's what
makes her music so magical.

00:12:02.040 --> 00:12:04.280
Is that she's allowing
people to be themselves

00:12:04.280 --> 00:12:06.140
within her world.

00:12:06.140 --> 00:12:09.830
- So timely, I think
one of her the hallmarks

00:12:09.830 --> 00:12:11.173
of her career up to this
point is collaboration.

00:12:11.173 --> 00:12:13.243
And that's what keeps her relevant.

00:12:13.243 --> 00:12:14.230
- She's so good at it.

00:12:14.230 --> 00:12:15.764
- She's looking for the next person.

00:12:15.764 --> 00:12:18.060
We got a question from the
audience I want to go through.

00:12:18.060 --> 00:12:20.250
They ask, is this the most
nervous you've ever been

00:12:20.250 --> 00:12:22.110
to work with an artist?

00:12:22.110 --> 00:12:26.950
- Probably so, I think this
was the most nervous I've been

00:12:32.180 --> 00:12:37.180
just because with her you
want to get it really right.

00:12:40.430 --> 00:12:44.096
It's her and Pharrell might
have been like the most

00:12:44.096 --> 00:12:46.800
because with them obviously
they're very busy people

00:12:46.800 --> 00:12:50.213
and you don't want to ever feel like

00:12:50.213 --> 00:12:52.133
you're wasting their time.

00:12:53.283 --> 00:12:56.640
And so yes, I was extremely nervous.

00:12:56.640 --> 00:12:58.220
- And audience keep questions coming.

00:12:58.220 --> 00:12:59.486
You can ask whatever you want.

00:12:59.486 --> 00:13:01.690
This is the conversation
of me and Dixon kick in.

00:13:01.690 --> 00:13:04.860
And I'm just gonna keep on
digging into this story.

00:13:04.860 --> 00:13:08.050
So she bet you keep tweaking
with it, keep pulling from it.

00:13:08.050 --> 00:13:10.410
When did you know this is it?

00:13:10.410 --> 00:13:13.040
This is the version.

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:17.270
So I played around with
many versions of this song.

00:13:17.270 --> 00:13:20.930
And I think the moment I knew
that it was the right one,

00:13:20.930 --> 00:13:24.220
I got a note from her team that said

00:13:24.220 --> 00:13:28.400
she might be interested in
going back a couple versions

00:13:28.400 --> 00:13:30.310
to the original one.

00:13:30.310 --> 00:13:34.010
And I was like okay, I've
done all these tweaks.

00:13:34.010 --> 00:13:37.423
And this is like just a message
for creatives, honestly.

00:13:38.860 --> 00:13:41.510
Sometimes your original
version is the version.

00:13:41.510 --> 00:13:42.343
- It's the one.

00:13:45.740 --> 00:13:48.540
- I can already see Kenny's
face off to the side.

00:13:48.540 --> 00:13:52.010
I know he's like for
real, that's how you feel?

00:13:52.010 --> 00:13:55.490
But sometimes your original
version is the version.

00:13:55.490 --> 00:13:57.360
That's what happened with this record.

00:13:57.360 --> 00:14:01.918
I think the intention
of the original demo,

00:14:01.918 --> 00:14:06.450
it was so straightforward and
straight ahead that anytime

00:14:06.450 --> 00:14:11.450
we tried to over embellish
something, it did too much.

00:14:15.420 --> 00:14:16.680
That's a message for creative.

00:14:16.680 --> 00:14:20.138
Sometimes you got it on the
first one and don't overthink it

00:14:20.138 --> 00:14:20.971
- Trust your instinct.

00:14:20.971 --> 00:14:22.640
- Yeah, trust your instinct.

00:14:22.640 --> 00:14:25.920
And then when you get
confirmation from somebody like B,

00:14:25.920 --> 00:14:30.920
that your instinct was right,
then you kind of feel good.

00:14:31.729 --> 00:14:32.960
- You're like see, I told you Kenny.

00:14:32.960 --> 00:14:35.100
All right, we got another
question from the audience.

00:14:35.100 --> 00:14:37.920
You work with the queen, who
do you wanna work with next?

00:14:37.920 --> 00:14:39.433
Can it get any better?

00:14:40.550 --> 00:14:42.400
- There's not really a better singer.

00:14:44.222 --> 00:14:46.220
But there are plenty of
artists I want to work with.

00:14:46.220 --> 00:14:49.216
I really wanna work with Kendrick.

00:14:49.216 --> 00:14:52.549
I think I wanna do
something really theatrical

00:14:52.549 --> 00:14:53.620
with Kendrick.

00:14:53.620 --> 00:14:57.300
Because I feel like he's such
a great performer obviously,

00:14:57.300 --> 00:15:00.140
but the way he delivers
things is really special.

00:15:00.140 --> 00:15:03.830
And I think that he has
so many different versions

00:15:03.830 --> 00:15:05.223
of himself as an artist.

00:15:05.223 --> 00:15:08.120
And I want to tap into
this more theatrical side

00:15:08.120 --> 00:15:08.953
of Kendrick.

00:15:08.953 --> 00:15:12.261
Obviously Rihanna is high on the list.

00:15:12.261 --> 00:15:14.910
I think we all just want
music from her honestly.

00:15:14.910 --> 00:15:15.743
- We all do.

00:15:15.743 --> 00:15:17.070
Can we get this dancehall album please?

00:15:17.070 --> 00:15:19.670
Can we get something, anything?

00:15:19.670 --> 00:15:23.810
- Yeah, Rihanna is a big one on the list.

00:15:23.810 --> 00:15:28.040
There's a lot of new artists
too that I really love.

00:15:28.040 --> 00:15:30.290
I'm a new artist myself.

00:15:30.290 --> 00:15:33.310
So I champion new artist.

00:15:33.310 --> 00:15:34.513
I love Cimino.

00:15:36.210 --> 00:15:41.210
I think Cimino is a prolific
writer and performer.

00:15:41.670 --> 00:15:44.311
I really love Lucky Daye.

00:15:44.311 --> 00:15:45.144
I think he's one.

00:15:45.144 --> 00:15:46.840
- Me too, I talk about
Lucky Daye all of the time.

00:15:46.840 --> 00:15:50.850
- He's one of the best writers
writing songs right now.

00:15:50.850 --> 00:15:51.880
- Loving the new album?

00:15:51.880 --> 00:15:52.890
- I do love the new album.

00:15:52.890 --> 00:15:53.723
- It's great.

00:15:56.820 --> 00:15:59.660
- As a writer, I get to hear demos a lot.

00:15:59.660 --> 00:16:00.930
Every time I hear a Lucky demo,

00:16:00.930 --> 00:16:04.150
I'm like this man is just so prolific.

00:16:04.150 --> 00:16:07.230
So I love Lucky, I hope one
day we get to work together

00:16:07.230 --> 00:16:08.663
and do some songs together.

00:16:09.580 --> 00:16:11.743
Yeah, I love her obviously.

00:16:12.940 --> 00:16:13.773
What a gift?

00:16:13.773 --> 00:16:14.606
What a talent?

00:16:15.440 --> 00:16:17.420
So the new artist get a lot of love too.

00:16:17.420 --> 00:16:19.926
Drake is big on my list
too because I feel like

00:16:19.926 --> 00:16:22.723
that's another artist who
has many versions of himself.

00:16:23.565 --> 00:16:24.780
- I mean he can do almost any style.

00:16:24.780 --> 00:16:25.613
- He really can.

00:16:25.613 --> 00:16:28.680
And Drake also can really sing,

00:16:28.680 --> 00:16:30.230
I don't care what anybody says.

00:16:31.386 --> 00:16:35.570
I love singing Drake just as
much as I love rapping Drake.

00:16:35.570 --> 00:16:38.797
So that would be really cool
to work with him for sure.

00:16:38.797 --> 00:16:40.393
- Do you feel like you made it?

00:16:42.360 --> 00:16:45.293
- I tend to not think about it often.

00:16:46.467 --> 00:16:47.534
Like I said, this is my job

00:16:47.534 --> 00:16:51.620
and like what a crazy job to have.

00:16:51.620 --> 00:16:55.410
You get to wake up every
morning and do exactly

00:16:55.410 --> 00:16:57.082
what you love doing.

00:16:57.082 --> 00:16:59.340
There's little things inside the job

00:16:59.340 --> 00:17:02.230
that give you certain
stresses and things like that

00:17:02.230 --> 00:17:04.560
- By definition of a job.

00:17:04.560 --> 00:17:05.460
Those things are gonna be there

00:17:05.460 --> 00:17:10.020
- Yeah but what a crazy life
I get to live as a musician,

00:17:10.020 --> 00:17:13.670
and a producer and I get paid for it.

00:17:13.670 --> 00:17:17.760
And you I can live the way
I've always dreamed living.

00:17:17.760 --> 00:17:19.440
So I don't think about making it.

00:17:19.440 --> 00:17:23.590
I think making it for me
is just being able to do

00:17:23.590 --> 00:17:25.780
what you love freely.

00:17:25.780 --> 00:17:28.460
And a lot of people don't
have that opportunity.

00:17:28.460 --> 00:17:32.220
And so I'm extremely grateful every day

00:17:32.220 --> 00:17:34.040
that I get to do this

00:17:34.040 --> 00:17:36.422
because a lot of people
don't get to do it.

00:17:36.422 --> 00:17:38.775
And there's a lot of people
that are high up at their jobs,

00:17:38.775 --> 00:17:40.570
but they're not doing what they love.

00:17:40.570 --> 00:17:41.453
- That's right.

00:17:41.453 --> 00:17:42.960
- But they do it because
they have to provide

00:17:42.960 --> 00:17:45.550
and they have to make life
happen for themselves.

00:17:45.550 --> 00:17:48.440
I'm just one of the lucky
ones that get to do it

00:17:48.440 --> 00:17:49.273
and it's exactly what I want to do it.

00:17:49.273 --> 00:17:51.182
- Sounds like you living in a moment.

00:17:51.182 --> 00:17:52.743
- Oh yeah, every day.

00:17:53.780 --> 00:17:55.960
- How'd you get to a place in your life

00:17:55.960 --> 00:17:58.170
where you could just live
in a moment like that.

00:17:58.170 --> 00:18:02.800
- I think in 2019, I
lost a lot of relatives.

00:18:02.800 --> 00:18:03.633
I lost my father,

00:18:03.633 --> 00:18:06.550
and my grandfather, my
brother and my sister

00:18:06.550 --> 00:18:08.143
all in one year.

00:18:08.143 --> 00:18:10.510
And I think that's when I was like,

00:18:10.510 --> 00:18:14.990
no, if I'm a live, I'm a live now.

00:18:14.990 --> 00:18:18.123
Because I don't want any regrets.

00:18:18.123 --> 00:18:21.700
And I remember, my father was diagnosed

00:18:21.700 --> 00:18:24.790
with cancer like three
years before he passed.

00:18:24.790 --> 00:18:28.030
So I got to spend three years literally

00:18:28.030 --> 00:18:29.340
just right next to of him.

00:18:29.340 --> 00:18:32.310
And I mean this is a guy
who accomplished quite a bit

00:18:32.310 --> 00:18:34.780
in his time on the earth.

00:18:34.780 --> 00:18:37.740
But there were still
moments that I could feel

00:18:37.740 --> 00:18:39.240
that he had regret.

00:18:39.240 --> 00:18:41.800
And he would constantly remind me,

00:18:41.800 --> 00:18:44.580
son if you're going to do something,

00:18:44.580 --> 00:18:47.010
don't do it with any regrets.

00:18:47.010 --> 00:18:52.010
Just go for it and live as
big and as bold as you can.

00:18:52.814 --> 00:18:55.940
And don't cower for anybody telling you,

00:18:55.940 --> 00:18:57.878
you can't do that.

00:18:57.878 --> 00:19:02.310
So that watching him go through that,

00:19:02.310 --> 00:19:05.750
I just decided for
myself I'm gonna do this.

00:19:05.750 --> 00:19:07.290
- Basically, you gonna
live while you here.

00:19:07.290 --> 00:19:09.970
- Got to, there's no other way to do it.

00:19:09.970 --> 00:19:14.053
Money is a tool, it comes and it goes.

00:19:15.060 --> 00:19:18.970
But your life is the most
valuable thing that you have,

00:19:18.970 --> 00:19:20.210
so you gotta live it.

00:19:20.210 --> 00:19:22.450
- I tell people the money will come.

00:19:22.450 --> 00:19:26.150
Go after what you love, make
sure you're eating every day.

00:19:26.150 --> 00:19:27.758
But the money will come.

00:19:27.758 --> 00:19:29.470
It'll all come together.

00:19:29.470 --> 00:19:31.770
I gotta question from a young songwriter,

00:19:31.770 --> 00:19:33.700
was there something you
wish someone told you

00:19:33.700 --> 00:19:36.010
at the beginning of your career?

00:19:36.010 --> 00:19:37.020
- As a songwriter,

00:19:37.020 --> 00:19:39.700
I wish someone would've told
me how valuable learning,

00:19:39.700 --> 00:19:41.833
how to produce was.

00:19:42.860 --> 00:19:45.510
Early on in my career, I
always was making my own beats.

00:19:45.510 --> 00:19:49.010
But I think if you're a
really good songwriter

00:19:49.010 --> 00:19:50.600
and people love what you do,

00:19:50.600 --> 00:19:52.797
they're gonna call you in
the room to do just that.

00:19:52.797 --> 00:19:54.604
And what ends up
happening in this business

00:19:54.604 --> 00:19:58.030
is that songwriters get stuck in a cadence

00:19:58.030 --> 00:19:59.573
of just being songwriters.

00:20:00.730 --> 00:20:03.800
- And not being able to shape
the whole sound of the song.

00:20:03.800 --> 00:20:05.440
- In a lot of ways they are shaping

00:20:05.440 --> 00:20:06.640
the whole sound of the song,

00:20:06.640 --> 00:20:09.650
but the title makes them
only one piece of it.

00:20:09.650 --> 00:20:13.230
So I think I wish someone had
told me earlier in my life

00:20:13.230 --> 00:20:17.000
that I could demand because I'm shaping

00:20:17.000 --> 00:20:19.900
the sound of the song vocally,

00:20:19.900 --> 00:20:21.750
when I could become a vocal producer.

00:20:24.702 --> 00:20:27.230
We obviously know the
industry works like this,

00:20:27.230 --> 00:20:29.403
the songwriters they get paid later.

00:20:31.150 --> 00:20:32.668
- Through the publishing .

00:20:32.668 --> 00:20:33.501
- Through the Publishing that's right.

00:20:33.501 --> 00:20:35.330
But producers get paid up front.

00:20:35.330 --> 00:20:37.160
Vocal producers get paid up front.

00:20:37.160 --> 00:20:39.003
So if you're a great
songwriter and you can sing

00:20:39.003 --> 00:20:41.377
and you're creating and
shaping these worlds

00:20:41.377 --> 00:20:43.300
with your melodies,

00:20:43.300 --> 00:20:48.283
the best thing I could tell
you is demand production.

00:20:49.268 --> 00:20:51.810
And I know it's not a popular take,

00:20:51.810 --> 00:20:53.490
but it's a hot take for me.

00:20:53.490 --> 00:20:54.848
I wish somebody would've told me

00:20:54.848 --> 00:20:58.945
when I was younger that I
could demand production credits

00:20:58.945 --> 00:21:01.200
for what I was doing.

00:21:01.200 --> 00:21:02.640
- All right, so I'm a breakfast person

00:21:02.640 --> 00:21:06.550
and the listeners out there
know I'm big with breakfast.

00:21:06.550 --> 00:21:08.543
No matter what time my day starts.

00:21:10.390 --> 00:21:12.010
I knew it as soon as I saw you.

00:21:12.010 --> 00:21:13.720
- Breakfast it's we just had it.

00:21:13.720 --> 00:21:15.770
- So you already know the answer to this,

00:21:17.210 --> 00:21:19.823
but the question is, does
Dixon eat French toast?

00:21:19.823 --> 00:21:21.920
Because I do have a fire recipe.

00:21:21.920 --> 00:21:23.610
- Yes, I do eat French toast

00:21:23.610 --> 00:21:25.666
more than I would like to admit.

00:21:25.666 --> 00:21:26.499
- I had some this morning.

00:21:26.499 --> 00:21:27.580
- I love French toast.

00:21:27.580 --> 00:21:29.740
I do not make great French toast.

00:21:29.740 --> 00:21:30.573
- I got you.

00:21:30.573 --> 00:21:31.830
- My girlfriend,

00:21:31.830 --> 00:21:32.663
- Hers though?

00:21:32.663 --> 00:21:33.540
- is out of control

00:21:35.260 --> 00:21:36.093
- you don't need me?

00:21:36.093 --> 00:21:37.076
- I mean you should just come over

00:21:37.076 --> 00:21:40.182
and then taste everybody's French toast.

00:21:40.182 --> 00:21:41.015
- Oh, let's do that.

00:21:41.015 --> 00:21:42.060
All right, let's set that up.

00:21:42.060 --> 00:21:44.160
I'm coming through, Kenny has my contacts.

00:21:45.560 --> 00:21:46.737
- Kenny likes French toast too.

00:21:46.737 --> 00:21:49.092
- Oh, I Know Kenny like toast.

00:21:49.092 --> 00:21:50.140
Kenny look like he like French toast?

00:21:50.140 --> 00:21:52.533
- Oh my God, we love breakfast.

00:21:53.799 --> 00:21:55.853
- What does success look like to you?

00:21:57.050 --> 00:22:01.520
- Success it's a very nuanced thing.

00:22:01.520 --> 00:22:05.760
Because on most days you just feel like

00:22:05.760 --> 00:22:09.252
you are working with your head down.

00:22:09.252 --> 00:22:11.670
Like that's what success feels like to me,

00:22:11.670 --> 00:22:13.270
getting the call.

00:22:13.270 --> 00:22:17.870
And people wanting me
around to do the thing

00:22:17.870 --> 00:22:20.923
that I'm called to do you.

00:22:21.980 --> 00:22:23.600
Honestly, that's what it feels like to me.

00:22:23.600 --> 00:22:25.850
Every time I get a call,

00:22:25.850 --> 00:22:28.170
or my management, or my
assistant gets a call

00:22:28.170 --> 00:22:30.279
and they're like, they
wanna work with you.

00:22:30.279 --> 00:22:32.326
That feels like success to me.

00:22:32.326 --> 00:22:34.500
Which is a different approach.

00:22:34.500 --> 00:22:35.960
I have a lot of friends in this business

00:22:35.960 --> 00:22:38.530
that look at success in a different way.

00:22:38.530 --> 00:22:43.360
But I think it's not just
about being in demand.

00:22:43.360 --> 00:22:48.360
It's I think people by virtue
of learning who you are

00:22:48.550 --> 00:22:51.750
as a creative when they
continue to call you

00:22:51.750 --> 00:22:53.160
to show up for things.

00:22:53.160 --> 00:22:55.060
That's success for me.

00:22:55.060 --> 00:22:59.160
Obviously the awards and
everything are a nice added bonus.

00:22:59.160 --> 00:23:02.363
But at the end of the day I
got in this business to work.

00:23:03.350 --> 00:23:07.330
- So when my phone is
ringing, I feel successful.

00:23:07.330 --> 00:23:08.990
- Yeah, because one thing
set up the next thing.

00:23:08.990 --> 00:23:11.800
And it shows you that
you're gonna keep working.

00:23:11.800 --> 00:23:13.160
It's a reminder that
I'm gonna keep working.

00:23:13.160 --> 00:23:16.173
- That this is my forever career

00:23:16.173 --> 00:23:18.810
and I don't mind taking breaks,

00:23:18.810 --> 00:23:22.163
but I rarely take them
because I love the work.

00:23:23.046 --> 00:23:24.640
And that's success for me.

00:23:24.640 --> 00:23:26.340
Just being constantly able to work.

00:23:26.340 --> 00:23:29.470
When I look at artists
who I'm obsessed with

00:23:29.470 --> 00:23:30.400
and that I love,

00:23:30.400 --> 00:23:32.800
they're constantly doing something.

00:23:32.800 --> 00:23:37.230
And they've learned how to
pivot and create a world

00:23:37.230 --> 00:23:38.783
for themselves where they can keep working

00:23:38.783 --> 00:23:40.640
and that's success for me.

00:23:40.640 --> 00:23:42.580
- Now we all need balance.

00:23:42.580 --> 00:23:45.310
And that's something I'm
personally working on that

00:23:45.310 --> 00:23:48.160
that work life balance it's hard.

00:23:48.160 --> 00:23:50.530
You mentioned that you
don't say yes to everything.

00:23:50.530 --> 00:23:51.777
If you're gonna work on something,

00:23:51.777 --> 00:23:52.873
you're gonna put your all into it.

00:23:52.873 --> 00:23:54.272
It's gonna get your focus.

00:23:54.272 --> 00:23:56.250
How do you know what to say no to?

00:23:56.250 --> 00:23:58.130
- Well I mean, not to be too deep

00:23:58.130 --> 00:23:58.963
but I generally pray about.

00:23:58.963 --> 00:24:00.650
- Just get out deep as we need to.

00:24:00.650 --> 00:24:03.620
- Yeah, I generally pray about things.

00:24:03.620 --> 00:24:04.453
- I do too.

00:24:04.453 --> 00:24:06.880
- Before I go into anything.

00:24:06.880 --> 00:24:07.840
Whether it's a deal,

00:24:07.840 --> 00:24:12.183
or it is a song, or whether
it's a block of time

00:24:12.183 --> 00:24:15.040
that I have to give up
to do something else..

00:24:15.040 --> 00:24:19.100
I honestly pray and then I seek guidance.

00:24:19.100 --> 00:24:21.600
So I mean, I've been very fortunate.

00:24:21.600 --> 00:24:24.400
I worked most of my career by myself.

00:24:24.400 --> 00:24:27.053
But this days I have a team of people.

00:24:29.012 --> 00:24:31.690
one person on my team, my manager Kenny

00:24:31.690 --> 00:24:33.680
has been in the industry for a long time.

00:24:33.680 --> 00:24:36.720
So I always seek guidance there.

00:24:36.720 --> 00:24:40.010
First and foremost, I have
a lawyer who's incredible.

00:24:40.010 --> 00:24:41.300
So I seek guidance on whether

00:24:41.300 --> 00:24:44.610
I'm gonna get stuck in something.

00:24:44.610 --> 00:24:48.030
Or whether we can move, pivot

00:24:48.030 --> 00:24:51.930
And then my assistant man is
one of the most creative people

00:24:51.930 --> 00:24:53.580
I've ever worked with.

00:24:53.580 --> 00:24:56.310
And he just always has sound advice

00:24:56.310 --> 00:24:58.908
about like my spirit.

00:24:58.908 --> 00:25:03.908
He's like, what tax is it
going to take on your spirit?

00:25:04.263 --> 00:25:05.842
- I love that question.

00:25:05.842 --> 00:25:08.930
- And so he's usually the
third person I talk to

00:25:08.930 --> 00:25:11.150
and he's like, do you
really want to do this?

00:25:11.150 --> 00:25:14.510
Or do you feel like you're
gonna be missing out?

00:25:14.510 --> 00:25:16.503
Because that FOMO is very real.

00:25:17.770 --> 00:25:19.560
You always have to take into consideration

00:25:19.560 --> 00:25:21.950
the tax something is
gonna take on your soul

00:25:21.950 --> 00:25:23.030
and your spirit.

00:25:23.030 --> 00:25:25.060
Talking with my wise counsel

00:25:25.920 --> 00:25:28.050
that's how I make decisions on things.

00:25:28.050 --> 00:25:31.610
I have tried a lot
especially in the last year,

00:25:31.610 --> 00:25:33.770
because things have just been really busy

00:25:33.770 --> 00:25:37.140
to not make decisions
completely on my own.

00:25:37.140 --> 00:25:38.326
That will derail something

00:25:38.326 --> 00:25:40.710
that might be happening
internally with my team.

00:25:40.710 --> 00:25:43.259
where I usually try to pick
up the phone and call somebody

00:25:43.259 --> 00:25:46.140
and be like, Hey this
is what's on the table.

00:25:46.140 --> 00:25:46.973
What should we do?

00:25:46.973 --> 00:25:49.510
Or Kenny calls me and
like, we got this thing.

00:25:49.510 --> 00:25:51.130
I'll talk to him about what he thinks,

00:25:51.130 --> 00:25:52.910
and then I'll pick up
the phone and call Khalil

00:25:52.910 --> 00:25:55.973
and he'll be like, I don't
know if that's for the brand.

00:25:57.930 --> 00:26:00.690
It's a nice working balance.

00:26:00.690 --> 00:26:03.442
And I just love that my
team is able to communicate

00:26:03.442 --> 00:26:05.463
with each other effectively,

00:26:05.463 --> 00:26:07.536
- They all work together
to achieve the same thing.

00:26:07.536 --> 00:26:11.210
- It gets tough when those
lines of communication

00:26:11.210 --> 00:26:14.213
aren't good or they start to get blurred.

00:26:15.990 --> 00:26:17.650
- Another audience question said,

00:26:17.650 --> 00:26:21.640
Kendrick is theatrical would
you do a musical with Kendrick?

00:26:21.640 --> 00:26:24.150
- Yes, Kendrick Lamar.

00:26:24.150 --> 00:26:25.076
- Kendrick, are you out there?

00:26:25.076 --> 00:26:26.170
- I'm gonna talk to him

00:26:26.170 --> 00:26:29.037
Whatever y'all are trying
to do as a musical,

00:26:29.037 --> 00:26:32.640
I'm fully there for it.

00:26:32.640 --> 00:26:36.863
I started my music career
as a songwriter and producer

00:26:36.863 --> 00:26:38.290
for musical theater.

00:26:38.290 --> 00:26:39.123
- Oh really?

00:26:39.123 --> 00:26:40.625
- Yeah, in Atlanta, Georgia.

00:26:40.625 --> 00:26:42.870
There's a lot of famous alumni from

00:26:42.870 --> 00:26:44.383
the Youth Ensemble of Atlanta.

00:26:45.340 --> 00:26:48.650
But I started there my
first gig as a producer.

00:26:48.650 --> 00:26:53.650
I was writing I think it
was 25 or 30 original songs

00:26:53.690 --> 00:26:58.130
for stage for a two hour long musical

00:26:58.130 --> 00:26:59.320
that debuted in Atlanta.

00:26:59.320 --> 00:27:01.500
And so I grew up in theater.

00:27:01.500 --> 00:27:04.600
So that's why I'm able to
pick that out from Kendrick.

00:27:04.600 --> 00:27:05.692
I'm like, oh my God.

00:27:05.692 --> 00:27:06.525
- You can see it.

00:27:06.525 --> 00:27:07.590
- I can see it.

00:27:07.590 --> 00:27:09.743
- So Kendrick, hi, I'm Dixon.

00:27:09.743 --> 00:27:11.300
- Yes Dixon, Kendrick.

00:27:11.300 --> 00:27:15.770
- Hello, I'm Oscar nominated
if that counts for anything.

00:27:15.770 --> 00:27:16.878
- I think it does.

00:27:16.878 --> 00:27:18.463
- But yeah, I would love to
do a musical with Kendrick.

00:27:18.463 --> 00:27:20.320
There's plenty of artists that I feel like

00:27:20.320 --> 00:27:24.400
would be really great
in a musical setting.

00:27:24.400 --> 00:27:26.320
Kendrick also sings too.

00:27:26.320 --> 00:27:29.410
He has a very special
tone in his singing voice,

00:27:29.410 --> 00:27:31.610
but I wouldn't just use him as a rapper.

00:27:31.610 --> 00:27:33.127
I would have to use all.

00:27:33.127 --> 00:27:35.850
- You really tap into all
of those different facets

00:27:35.850 --> 00:27:37.040
of his artistry.

00:27:37.040 --> 00:27:38.770
I wanna ask you some a
couple of fun questions

00:27:38.770 --> 00:27:41.080
before I let you outta here Dixon.

00:27:41.080 --> 00:27:42.362
What do you say?

00:27:42.362 --> 00:27:45.660
I mean talking to Oscar's
celebration of movies,

00:27:45.660 --> 00:27:48.040
we focus on the awards
when we go to Oscars

00:27:48.040 --> 00:27:52.290
and rightfully so, but it's
really a celebration of film.

00:27:52.290 --> 00:27:56.683
What is your favorite film
soundtrack of all time?

00:27:58.290 --> 00:27:59.300
Or you can name off a couple.

00:27:59.300 --> 00:28:01.990
- Okay cool, I was about
to say, I can't really do.

00:28:01.990 --> 00:28:03.703
- Yeah, name off a couple.

00:28:04.800 --> 00:28:06.963
- I really love School Daze.

00:28:08.802 --> 00:28:11.140
It's different.

00:28:11.140 --> 00:28:14.540
And there's like the
singles from School Daze.

00:28:14.540 --> 00:28:16.590
But the stuff that Dr. Bill Lee

00:28:16.590 --> 00:28:19.941
was doing just in the score.

00:28:19.941 --> 00:28:21.320
- Spike Lee's father.

00:28:21.320 --> 00:28:24.430
- Spike Lee's father,
composer, genius musician.

00:28:24.430 --> 00:28:26.900
The stuff that he was doing in the score

00:28:26.900 --> 00:28:29.162
was really special.

00:28:29.162 --> 00:28:31.600
The openings of the Wake up scene.

00:28:31.600 --> 00:28:33.420
it's the opening scene on the soundtrack,

00:28:33.420 --> 00:28:36.000
but it's the last scene in the film.

00:28:36.000 --> 00:28:38.760
It's one of the most
beautiful pieces of music

00:28:38.760 --> 00:28:40.419
I've ever heard in film.

00:28:40.419 --> 00:28:41.433
So that's School Daze.

00:28:41.433 --> 00:28:43.100
And then one that
doesn't get talked about,

00:28:43.100 --> 00:28:47.230
both of mine are spike
Lee, by the way Bamboozled.

00:28:47.230 --> 00:28:49.240
- Bamboozled, the mal mouse.

00:28:49.240 --> 00:28:54.240
- Jesus Christ Erykah Badu does Hollywood,

00:28:55.790 --> 00:28:56.863
the Chaka Khan cover.

00:28:57.780 --> 00:28:59.260
It's a crazy soundtrack,

00:28:59.260 --> 00:29:02.280
but then to watch the film
and see it in real time.

00:29:02.280 --> 00:29:04.220
Also one of my really close friends

00:29:04.220 --> 00:29:06.600
who I grew up in theater with his father

00:29:06.600 --> 00:29:09.640
was in the film Bamboozled
Thomas Byrd Jefferson who passed.

00:29:09.640 --> 00:29:13.050
- Oh yeah, rest in power, great actor.

00:29:13.050 --> 00:29:16.240
- Amazing actor, man everything.

00:29:16.240 --> 00:29:19.080
- Everything, he always stole a show.

00:29:19.080 --> 00:29:20.420
Whenever he came on screen.

00:29:20.420 --> 00:29:21.397
- An incredible actor.

00:29:21.397 --> 00:29:22.230
- You believed him.

00:29:22.230 --> 00:29:23.770
- I believed everything he says.

00:29:23.770 --> 00:29:26.540
Even in life and as meeting him in person.

00:29:26.540 --> 00:29:27.408
- I'm a little jealous.

00:29:27.408 --> 00:29:30.140
- Just an incredible actor.

00:29:30.140 --> 00:29:32.530
Also Savion Glover is in Bamboozled

00:29:32.530 --> 00:29:35.680
and the music he's creating
with his feet alone

00:29:35.680 --> 00:29:37.870
is just it's insane.

00:29:37.870 --> 00:29:42.630
So those are two films whose
soundtrack means a lot to me.

00:29:42.630 --> 00:29:44.590
- Those are great choices.

00:29:44.590 --> 00:29:46.540
- Also honorable mentioned Wayne Casey.

00:29:50.267 --> 00:29:53.102
Probably a soundtrack with
the most singles outside

00:29:53.102 --> 00:29:55.880
of Car Wash, which is Rose Royce.

00:29:55.880 --> 00:29:59.420
My grandfather worked on Car Wash as a DP

00:29:59.420 --> 00:30:00.253
and cinematographer.

00:30:00.253 --> 00:30:05.020
So those two are my honorable mentions.

00:30:05.020 --> 00:30:08.100
Because those are soundtracks
that have the most singles.

00:30:08.100 --> 00:30:09.210
- You could just put them on.

00:30:09.210 --> 00:30:11.810
And everybody knows the
songs, which is crazy.

00:30:11.810 --> 00:30:13.890
I was just listening to
the Car Wash soundtrack

00:30:13.890 --> 00:30:14.723
the other day.

00:30:14.723 --> 00:30:17.710
I was like man, this thing had
like five or six singles off.

00:30:17.710 --> 00:30:19.777
- Which is insane.

00:30:19.777 --> 00:30:21.590
- Which means the movie was a hit,

00:30:21.590 --> 00:30:23.240
no matter what the industry says.

00:30:24.550 --> 00:30:25.540
The movie was a hit

00:30:25.540 --> 00:30:27.170
like people loved that music.
- And definitely

00:30:27.170 --> 00:30:28.520
a culture bear.

00:30:28.520 --> 00:30:29.981
You know what I mean?

00:30:29.981 --> 00:30:32.612
Something that impacted
and shaped the culture.

00:30:32.612 --> 00:30:36.410
What's the first album that
you paid for with your money

00:30:36.410 --> 00:30:38.710
or that you just had to have for yourself.

00:30:38.710 --> 00:30:41.040
- You're gonna freak out when I say this.

00:30:41.040 --> 00:30:42.990
The first album I ever bought,

00:30:42.990 --> 00:30:46.666
my mom had this like CD
subscription thing back in the day.

00:30:46.666 --> 00:30:48.206
- Like the RCA Columbia joint?

00:30:48.206 --> 00:30:49.039
- Sure it did.

00:30:49.039 --> 00:30:52.520
And the first album I
ever received in the mail

00:30:52.520 --> 00:30:55.900
was Destiny's Child, The
Writing's On The Wall.

00:30:55.900 --> 00:30:57.877
- I mean The Writing's On The Wall.

00:30:57.877 --> 00:30:58.900
- No, it's kind of crazy.

00:30:58.900 --> 00:31:00.900
I think about it all the time.

00:31:00.900 --> 00:31:02.700
- Pretty nuts, did you tell Beyonce that?

00:31:02.700 --> 00:31:03.533
- I haven't.

00:31:03.533 --> 00:31:05.120
I told Kenny that one time,

00:31:05.120 --> 00:31:06.403
I was like the first album I ever bought

00:31:06.403 --> 00:31:07.540
with my own money was

00:31:07.540 --> 00:31:10.160
The Writing's On The Wall, it's crazy.

00:31:10.160 --> 00:31:13.403
- I mean I'm nearly speechless from that.

00:31:14.930 --> 00:31:17.220
- I asked my mom the other
day if she remembered

00:31:17.220 --> 00:31:20.313
the CD subscription because
I was obsessed with it.

00:31:20.313 --> 00:31:23.867
- Yeah, because you get
like 10 or 12 a month.

00:31:23.867 --> 00:31:27.750
- What was crazy about this
one is that it was like,

00:31:27.750 --> 00:31:29.950
I got my subscription was a little smaller

00:31:29.950 --> 00:31:32.070
because I didn't have that much money.

00:31:32.070 --> 00:31:34.290
But I would get like a CD every month.

00:31:34.290 --> 00:31:36.230
- Wow, that's good.

00:31:36.230 --> 00:31:38.960
The first one that ever came

00:31:38.960 --> 00:31:39.970
- Was The Writing's On The Wall.

00:31:39.970 --> 00:31:42.660
- I mean I think on that note,
you gotta end it on that.

00:31:42.660 --> 00:31:45.763
Dixon, congratulations God bless you.

00:31:45.763 --> 00:31:48.740
I wish you all the best then
I'm gonna see you Sunday

00:31:48.740 --> 00:31:49.704
and then we gonna get up
for that French toast.

00:31:49.704 --> 00:31:50.783
- Oh yeah we got to, French toast.

00:31:53.130 --> 00:31:54.080
- Peace

00:31:54.080 --> 00:31:54.961
- Thanks Bro.

00:31:54.961 --> 00:31:57.544
(upbeat music)

